Chapter: CoapClient
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Encode chapter CoapEndpoint
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A CoAP client on a real socket, so that something other than our own
 loopback can be the thing that answers it.

 `codex/test/apps/coap-loopback` drives `CoapEndpoint` against datagrams
 built by our own `Coap` codec. That proves the endpoint and the codec
 agree with each other, which is the failure mode
 `docs/PM/Active/Stories/BrotliBeatsOpus.md` exists to describe: two
 halves that share an assumption agree forever. This binary exists to be
 answered by aiocoap, which agrees with RFC 7252 instead.

 It asks for two resources and reports what came back for each. The
 second is asked for precisely because it is NOT there: a client that
 cannot tell 2.05 Content from 4.04 Not Found would pass a test that only
 ever fetched something that exists, and "the response code is parsed" is
 otherwise a claim nothing checks.

 THE CLOCK IS THE PIT TICK. `CoapEndpoint` takes `now` as a caller-supplied
 number in the same unit as its timeouts, and `get-ticks` counts the
 periodic timer at roughly 18.2 Hz, so a tick is about 55 ms. That is the
 real clock this machine has; the endpoint's retransmission schedule is
 therefore real time and not a loop counter. On hardware whose PIT limps
 -- the ASUS in `docs/PM/Active/Stories/TheSilentKeyboard.md` runs it at
 about 3 Hz -- the timeouts stretch, which is a property of the clock and
 not of this code.

Section: Configuration

  coap-local-mac : List Integer = net-driver-mac
  coap-local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  coap-gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]

  coap-local-port : Integer = 5900
  coap-server-port : Integer = 5683

 Milliseconds per PIT tick, and the poll budget between clock reads. The
 budget is what makes a retransmission possible at all: the endpoint only
 retransmits when it is ticked past its deadline, so the receive poll has
 to give up periodically rather than block for the whole interaction.

  coap-ms-per-tick : Integer = 55
  coap-poll-fuel : Integer = 400000
  coap-rounds : Integer = 400

  coap-now : Integer -> Integer
  coap-now (x) = get-ticks * coap-ms-per-tick

Section: The Exchange

  CoapRun = record {
    cr-ep : CoapEp,
    cr-rounds : Integer
  }

 Put every datagram the endpoint asked for on the wire. A step can emit
 more than one -- a separate response is acknowledged while the answer is
 being delivered -- so this is a loop and not a send.

  coap-send-all : UdpSocket, List (List Integer), Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  coap-send-all (sk) (outs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length outs then 0
    else act
      sent <- udp-io-send sk (list-at outs i)
      rest <- coap-send-all sk outs (i + 1)
      sent + rest
    end

 One round: wait for a datagram, and if none comes before the poll budget
 is spent, advance the endpoint's clock instead. Those are the only two
 things that can move a CoAP interaction forward, and both are here so
 that a lost request is retransmitted by the same loop that would have
 delivered the answer.

  coap-run : UdpSocket, CoapEp, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] CoapEp
  coap-run (sk) (ep) (rounds) =
    if rounds <= 0 then ep
    else if coap-ep-done ep then ep
    else if coap-ep-failed ep then ep
    else act
      r <- udp-io-recv sk coap-poll-fuel
      let step = if r.ur-got then coap-ep-recv ep (r.ur-data) (coap-now 0)
                 else coap-ep-tick ep (coap-now 0)
      in act
        sent <- coap-send-all sk (step.cs-out) 0
        coap-run sk (step.cs-ep) (rounds - 1)
      end
    end

 ONE ENDPOINT SERVES BOTH REQUESTS, and it must. A `CoapEp` carries the
 message-id and token counters, so building a fresh one per request
 restarts both at 1 -- and RFC 7252 section 4.5 requires a server to treat
 a message id it has already seen from the same source as a DUPLICATE and
 replay the response it cached. That is what aiocoap did on the first run
 of `build/coap-interop-test.ps1`: the second request, for a resource that
 does not exist, came back 2.05 Content carrying the first resource's
 payload. The server was right and the client was wrong, and the loopback
 could never have shown it because nothing in the loopback caches.

  coap-ask : UdpSocket, CoapEp, Text -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] CoapEp
  coap-ask (sk) (ep) (path) =
    let step = coap-ep-get ep path (coap-now 0)
    in act
      sent <- coap-send-all sk (step.cs-out) 0
      coap-run sk (step.cs-ep) coap-rounds
    end

Section: Reporting

 The payload is reported as its bytes rather than as text. It arrives as
 whatever the server sent, and Codex Text is CCE internally -- rendering
 it would put a second encoding between the wire and the assertion, which
 is exactly where this project has lost bytes before. The harness knows
 what it asked the server to serve and computes the same list.

  coap-bytes-text : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  coap-bytes-text (bs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length bs then acc
    else coap-bytes-text bs (i + 1) (if i == 0 then show (list-at bs i) else acc & "," & show (list-at bs i))

  coap-report : Text, CoapEp -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  coap-report (label) (ep) = act
    print-line-uni ("coap " & label
      & " done=" & show (coap-ep-done ep)
      & " failed=" & show (coap-ep-failed ep)
      & " code=" & show (coap-ep-response-code ep)
      & " retries=" & show (coap-ep-retries ep)
      & " payload=" & coap-bytes-text (coap-ep-response-payload ep) 0 "")
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let sk = udp-io-open coap-local-mac coap-local-ip coap-local-port coap-gateway-ip coap-server-port
    in act
      hit <- coap-ask sk coap-ep-new "codex"
      coap-report "hit" hit
      miss <- coap-ask sk hit "nosuch"
      coap-report "miss" miss
    end
  end
